From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 19:45:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] dt-bindings: gpio: apple,d2365-pmic-gpio: Add the D2089 event
 controller

The iPad Air's Dialog D2089 PMIC (A7) latches its PMU events in the
same block as the D2207: iOS 12 AppleD2089PMU reads 14 bytes at 0x50 in
its interrupt handler, writes all ones to the 14 mask bytes at 0x70 at
start and subscribes events by number (ADC conversion complete is event
89 on both). Add apple,d2089-pmic-gpio for that event controller.

Its GPIO lines are not described: which events they raise is not known,
so a D2089 node is an interrupt controller only, with its PMU interrupt
required and no gpio-controller.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 .../bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml  | 31 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
index ed3f849fc..1a5a8c167 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml
@@ -4,7 +4,7 @@
 $id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/gpio/apple,d2365-pmic-gpio.yaml#
 $schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
 
-title: Apple A8/A9/A10 PMIC GPIO and event interrupt controller
+title: Apple A7/A8/A9/A10 PMIC GPIO and event interrupt controller
 
 maintainers:
   - Paul Praschl <praschlpaul@g-p.at>
@@ -25,8 +25,13 @@ description: |
   GPIO event is the line configuration left by the boot firmware, so only
   edge flags are accepted.
 
-  Interrupt 112 (D2207) or 240 (D2365), one past the last event, is the
-  event line itself. Its consumers run on every assertion of the line,
+  The Dialog D2089 PMIC used with the Apple A7 latches the same 112 events
+  in the same block as the D2207. Only that event controller is described:
+  its GPIO lines and the events they raise are not, so the node is no GPIO
+  controller.
+
+  Interrupt 112 (D2089, D2207) or 240 (D2365), one past the last event, is
+  the event line itself. Its consumers run on every assertion of the line,
   before the event block is read, so that a consumer can act before the I2C
   read that tells which event it was (the Lightning charger withdraws its
   input-current grant there).
@@ -34,6 +39,7 @@ description: |
 properties:
   compatible:
     enum:
+      - apple,d2089-pmic-gpio
       - apple,d2186-pmic-gpio
       - apple,d2207-pmic-gpio
       - apple,d2333-pmic-gpio
@@ -74,14 +80,29 @@ properties:
 
 required:
   - compatible
-  - gpio-controller
-  - "#gpio-cells"
 
 dependencies:
   interrupt-controller: ["#interrupt-cells"]
   apple,wake-events: [interrupt-controller]
 
 allOf:
+  - if:
+      properties:
+        compatible:
+          const: apple,d2089-pmic-gpio
+    then:
+      properties:
+        gpio-controller: false
+        "#gpio-cells": false
+        gpio-reserved-ranges: false
+        apple,wake-events: false
+      required:
+        - interrupt-controller
+        - interrupts
+    else:
+      required:
+        - gpio-controller
+        - "#gpio-cells"
   - if:
       properties:
         compatible:
